WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.645
Every Flask route is a boundary between
outside input and server-side behavior.

2
00:00:08.725 --> 00:00:16.630
Request data may arrive through path
parameters, query strings, form fields,

3
00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:24.136
JSON bodies, headers, cookies, file
uploads, and third-party webhooks.

4
00:00:24.216 --> 00:00:28.049
Some of that data comes from a browser.

5
00:00:28.129 --> 00:00:36.514
Some comes from scripts, mobile clients,
partners, queues, or automated systems.

6
00:00:36.594 --> 00:00:39.588
The secure default is simple:

7
00:00:39.668 --> 00:00:48.172
treat request data as untrusted until it
has been validated for the specific purpose

8
00:00:48.252 --> 00:00:49.690
it will serve.

9
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:55.160
Validation is more than checking that a
field exists.

10
00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:03.385
Good validation considers type, shape,
range, length, format, allowed values,

11
00:01:03.465 --> 00:01:05.621
and business intent.

12
00:01:05.701 --> 00:01:13.965
A customer identifier may be the correct
type and still refer to a record the caller

13
00:01:14.045 --> 00:01:15.962
should not access.

14
00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:23.708
A date may parse correctly and still be
outside the allowed business window.

15
00:01:23.788 --> 00:01:32.412
A webhook may have the expected JSON
structure and still need source authentication,

16
00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:37.882
replay protection, and a clear processing
contract.

17
00:01:37.962 --> 00:01:44.789
Schema validation can help teams make
request contracts explicit.

18
00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:52.535
A schema can define required fields,
optional fields, nesting, data types,

19
00:01:52.615 --> 00:01:56.807
length constraints, and allowed values.

20
00:01:56.887 --> 00:02:04.193
That does not remove the need for
authorization or business rule checks,

21
00:02:04.273 --> 00:02:12.179
but it reduces ambiguity and keeps route
handlers from becoming a pile of ad hoc

22
00:02:12.259 --> 00:02:13.696
assumptions.

23
00:02:13.776 --> 00:02:21.202
Size limits are also part of validation
because unbounded bodies, forms,

24
00:02:21.282 --> 00:02:29.667
and uploads can affect memory, storage,
parsing time, and downstream processing.

25
00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:34.658
Safe error responses are part of input
handling.

26
00:02:34.738 --> 00:02:41.685
A client should receive enough information
to correct a bad request,

27
00:02:41.765 --> 00:02:49.071
but not raw stack traces, SQL fragments,
secret values, internal paths,

28
00:02:49.151 --> 00:02:51.906
or framework diagnostics.

29
00:02:51.986 --> 00:02:59.652
For developers, the most useful pattern is
consistency: parse the request,

30
00:02:59.732 --> 00:03:06.320
validate its shape, enforce authorization,
perform the action,

31
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.873
and return a controlled response.

32
00:03:09.953 --> 00:03:17.140
That structure makes Flask routes easier
to review and safer to change.
